            Examples of twisted smooth cohomology in string theory
          %blockquote
            %p
              This section originates in notes prepared along with a lecture series (
              %a{:href => "#SchreiberLect"}> Schreiber ESI 2012
              ) at the Erwin-Schrödinger institute in 2012.
          %p This section discusses four classes of examples of twisted smooth cohomology in string theory
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#VielbeinFields"} Geometric structure (generalized, exceptional)
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#SpinStringFivebraneStructures"} Spin-, String-, and Fivebrane structure
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#TwistedK"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Spin
                            %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin^c
                    \-,
                    = succeed "-," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi String
                              %mi c
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} String^c
                    = succeed "-structure" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi Fivebrane
                              %mi c
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fivebrane^c
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#HigherOrientifold"} Higher orientifold structure
          %h3#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+fields"} background gauge fields
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            appearing in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            are mathematically described by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential
            refinements of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted K-theory
            that describes the
            = succeed "-twisted" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+fields"} Yang-Mills fields
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
          %p
            This page provides a survey of and introduction to such examples, organized along a
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#TableOfTwists"} Table of twists
            %em
              %a{:href => "#References"} references
            below.
          %p We start with an introduction to the general notion of twisted smooth cohomology by way of the simple but instructive class of examples of
          %ul
            %li
              %strong I)
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#VielbeinFields"} Geometric structure (generalized, exceptional)
          %p
            via
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduction+of+structure+groups"} reduction of structure groups
            %em smooth
            cohomology in addition to both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} ordinary topological/homotopical
            as well as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
          %p
            (The mathematically inclined reader wishing to see a more formal development of the general theory behind the discussion here should look at the section
            %em
              %a{:href => "#GeneralTheory"} General theory
            below for pointers.)
          %p
            Then we proceed in direct analogy, but now with ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
            generalized to the genuine
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+fields"} higher gauge fields
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %ul
            %li
              II)
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#SpinStringFivebraneStructures"} Spin-, String-, and Fivebrane structure
          %p
            Then we roughly indicate the relation between higher gauge fields and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomalies"} quantum anomalies
          %ul
            %li
              Interlude)
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#Anomalies"} Anomaly line bundle on smooth moduli stacks of fields
          %p
            Finally we put the pieces together and scan through various situations appearing in string theory with their anomaly structure and discuss the smooth moduli
            = succeed "-stacks" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li
              III)
              %em
                %a{:href => "#TwistedK"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Spin
                          %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin^c
                  \-,
                  = succeed "-," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi String
                            %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} String^c
                  = succeed "-structure" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Fivebrane
                            %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fivebrane^c
              \.
          %p There are various further examples. As an outlook we indicate aspects of
          %ul
            %li
              IV)
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#HigherOrientifold"} Higher orientifold structure
          %h3#TableOfTwists Overview: The Table of Twists
          %p
            The following sections discuss classes of examples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+differential+c-structures"} twisted smooth structures
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            refined to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth cohomology
            (a gentle explanation/example follows
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#VielbeinFields"} in a moment
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#TwistedCohomology"} below
            %em universal local coefficient bundle
          .maruku-equation
