          %p
            This now identifies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} lamda
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                      %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lamda_{i j k})
            with a cocycle in degree-2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+cohomology"} Cech cohomology
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                      %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mi smooth
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [(\lambda_{i j k})] \in H^2_{smooth}(X, U(1))
                  \\,.
          %p
            This classifies a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle 2-bundle
            \/
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} bundle gerbe
          %p Generally we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mi smooth
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi_0 \mathbf{H}(X, \mathbf{B}^n U(1))
                  \\simeq
                  H^n_{smooth}(X, U(1))
          %p
            and for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a smooth manifold
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots \simeq H^{n+1}(X, \mathbb{Z})
                  \\,.
          %p
            So apparently the smooth
            = succeed "-stack" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}^n U(1)
            is a
            %em smooth refinement
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+space"} Eilenberg-MacLane space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\mathbb{Z},n+1)
          %p This is made precise as follows.
          %p
            %strong Theorem
            There is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo :
                  %mi Smooth
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi Π
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  {\vert-\vert}
                  \:
                  Smooth\infty Grpd
                  \\stackrel{\Pi}{\to}
                  \\infty Grpd
                  \\stackrel{{\vert-\vert}}{\to}
                  Top
          %p
            which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} adjoint
            to the functor that assigns
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+%E2%88%9E-stacks"} constant ∞-stacks
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth ∞-groupoids
          %p
            We say that a choice of lift of a diagram of bare homotopy types through geometric realization is a smooth
            = succeed "." do
              %em geomtric refinement
          %p For instance one finds
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
